<h2>What this meta extractor checks</h2><p>The extractor reads the public HTML response and pulls out common SEO and sharing signals: the document title, meta description, canonical URL, robots meta tag, language, viewport, H1 count, heading hierarchy, Open Graph properties and Twitter card properties.</p>
<h2>How to use it</h2><ol><li>Paste a full public URL, including <code>https://</code>.</li><li>Run the extractor and review the grouped findings.</li><li>Fix missing or inconsistent signals on the source page, then run the check again.</li></ol>
<h2>Important interpretation notes</h2><p>A missing tag is a technical finding, not an automatic ranking penalty. A canonical declaration is also only a preference signal: search engines may choose a different canonical when their systems find another URL more representative. citeturn112108search3</p>
<h2>What this tool does not do</h2><p>It does not run a full crawl, evaluate Core Web Vitals, inspect server logs, prove indexability, or predict rankings. It is designed as a fast page-level metadata inspection tool.</p>
